as many things about this story in this  role that that were very dear to me and  sort of close close to me when I  embarked on the journey of creating this  character but one of the most glaring  sort of obstacles was the fact that i'm  not in fact from india i haven't lived  there extensively I my father's Indian  but my mother's Italian and I grew up in  London so I knew that there was a  certain amount of work that I would need  to do in order to be able to portray  this sort of iconic Bombay boy Salim and  so yeah I went to India first for about  a month in the late summer of two  thousand nine we ended up we shot in  2010 from februari through May pretty  much on a crazy shoot with 65 unit  shifts in 70 days it was a it was a real  real rollercoaster but I spent a month  in india first studying hindi traveling  around bombay and sort of really trying  to you know in a way develop a  storehouse of memories or of like  experiences because i had never been on  my own terms sort of in india i'd been  there with parents and for family  occasions stuff like that so yeah so um  I was there for a month and then went  back about two three months before we  started shooting and traveled quite a  bit and spent a lot of time studying  Hindi and walking around and watching  movies and reading everything about the  history which was another thing that I  was sort of vaguely you know I was  familiar with loosely but unsurprisingly  growing up in England they don't teach  you all that much about Indian history  so I I that was a big sort of crash  course as well academically in a way and  I read you know lots of books both about  partition and you know leading up to  partition but also and sometimes even  more interestingly about India in the  last sort of 60 years since independence  so so yeah it was a large large research  mission really to try and get inside the  head of this character initially I was  familiar with the novel I had read it  when  quite young but it was it wasn't I  hadn't read it sort of right before  meeting with Deepa certainly when we  first met it was more of a social the  social meeting I was in Toronto working  on another film and we had recently done  a play with someone she had worked with  and so we just sort of met as you do and  went out for lunch um but then once it  became clear to me that she was  considering me for this part I I drove  back into the book and sort of  coincidentally i started reading it on  rereading it on like a friday and i got  a call friday afternoon saying that i'd  be meeting with salman on monday so i  was like oh my god 600 pages in one  weekend but i like burned through the  whole thing again and and so yeah I  spoke with someone then and at that  point they was really it was you know  over a year and a half before we started  shooting so they were still working on  the script they even actually had I  think they had just decided not to make  it to films but the one of the original  sort of thoughts was to have it be a two  part movie and so then we spoke we spoke  at length about sort of our own  relationship to Bombay and you know  different families and the process of  that adaptation and that was pretty much  the only time we spoke we emailed a  little bit but then pretty soon  afterwards it became sort of clear that  my channel of discussion was deeper and  I spoke to her a lot and she was in  touch with Salman but he wasn't like you  know dealing day-to-day with the actors  at all and wasn't on set and so then I  would go back to the book I mean  constantly I had it you know have this  like threadbare copy that would  practically sleep on you know sleep with  it under my pillow I have always always  attached to it yeah they've really you  know deeper and someone and you know  Colin who's the editor have really  managed to sort of keep the in a way the  more historical moments feel I sometimes  feel like the more historical moments  feel a little bit more immediate and  personal for example I think about that  shot of General Zulfikar right before he  surrenders wiping his shoe you know in a  small room and some of the  emotional interpersonal moments are  treated in a more epic way you know  you've got the you know lovers reuniting  in the rain which is like an old sort of  deep azam aaj to like an old bollywood  trope which is the lovers sort of  falling in love and crying and kissing  in the rain so I think that yeah you  know I mean one we screen this film at  Telluride almost a year ago a little  under but um and you know terrorists up  one Street so you can't help but hear  what everybody's saying about all the  movies is you're like walking around and  I heard somebody say that it was like  the indian forest gump so i think that  that that is a film that also sort of  ties together certain aspects of a  country's history and also the history  of a character
 

